Quoting  sjdevnull@yahoo.com <sjdevnull@yahoo.com>:
>David Damerell wrote:
>>Quoting  sjdevnull@yahoo.com <sjdevnull@yahoo.com>:
>>>Probably protection, it's 3 extra points of AC and is one of the better
>>>long-term cloaks (along with MR, oilskin, and protection).
>>Oilskin is about top of the list of things not to wear all the time but
>>just to keep around for special circumstances.
>You don't always have time to swap it on/off or want to waste those
>turns.

You do against the only serious threat it deals with. Yes, grabbing
monsters come up _sometimes_, but it's not exactly threat number one (and
displacement might save you).

>it boils down to the old active/passive defense question and whether
>you're the type to remember to do the right thing all the time or
>whether keeping the defenses in place helps your chances of survival.

I don't think that's a question at all. We're not talking about "all the
time", of course; we're talking about certain rare and immediately obvious
situations, most of which occur at predictable points in the dungeon,
which can be defused of all danger with readily available resources.

If someone doesn't remember not to go near the water, the answer is for
them to work on that, not to permanently tie up the cloak spot working
around so simple an issue. If someone doesn't notice "Weak" on the status
line and keeps dying of hunger, the answer isn't "always play Healers and
huge-chunk every boulder you find"; it's "learn to watch the status line".

>>Only if it's MR. The MC-able problems in the early game just aren't that
>>common.
>MC prevents fatal poison attacks,

That is a point, although one kind of ranger who starts with a cloak of
displacement also starts poison resistant. Yes; if you are not poison
resistant, you are right to favour an MC3 cloak.

>>>Displacement is just too unpredictable to really rely on,
>>Sometimes it works. Sometimes you get hit. What's the difference between
>>that and extra AC points?
>Extra AC also helps take off damage if you do get hit (once you get a
>reasonable AC).

Yes, yes, I know that. The point is, your criticism of displacement is
that it is unpredictable. Your proposed alternative, a little extra AC, is
also unpredictable and of lesser effect.

>>Why step on the drawbridge death spot at all?
>That's not the problem, the problem is that you are standing a knight's
>move across the drawbridge using it to guillotine people and some
>soldier zaps a /striking (thinking you're right in front of the bridge)
>and blows up the bridge.

Now all the monsters are stuck on the other side of the moat making them
easy prey for ranged attacks. What's the problem? If a few extra moves
(and not squashing all their loot) bugs you so much, take off displacement
when you're at a drawbridge, a case that comes up once or twice per game.

>> >be a big problem.  Plus, once you're invisible it's completely useless.
>>Don't become permanently invisible.
>Someone else pointed out that it still works on monsters that see
>invisible anyway.

I know that, too. "Plus, once you're invisible it's completely useless" is
both false for some monsters and, to the extent it is true, doesn't
represent an actual problem.
